.TH PING 1 2026-10-27 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
ping, ping6 \- send ICMP echo requests to a network host
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ping
.RI [ options ]
.I host
.br
.B ping6
.RI [ options ]
.I host
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ping
sends ICMP echo requests to
.IR host ,
one a second, and prints a line for each echo reply that comes back.
When it stops, it prints statistics: how many requests were sent, how
many replies arrived, and the round-trip times.
This
.B ping
is a busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
.B ping6
is
.B ping
with
.BR \-6 :
it always uses IPv6, and
.B \-4
has no effect on it.
.SS Host
.I host
is a name, a dotted IPv4 address, or an IPv6 address.
Windows resolves a name.
When the name has several addresses,
.B ping
uses only one: the first IPv4 address, or else the first address.
With
.B \-4
or
.BR \-6 ,
only addresses of that family are considered, and a host that has none
is an error.
.PP
A port after a colon, as in
.BR example.com:80 ,
or brackets around an IPv6 address, as in
.BR [::1] ,
are accepted, and the port is ignored.
.SS Stopping
Without
.B \-c
or
.BR \-w ,
.B ping
runs until it is interrupted with Ctrl-C.
.PP
With
.B \-c
.IR count ,
it sends
.I count
requests and exits as soon as
.I count
replies have arrived.
Otherwise it waits after the last request: about twice the longest
round-trip time so far, and at least 1 second, if any reply has
arrived, or else the time set by
.BR \-W ,
10 seconds by default.
.PP
With
.B \-w
.IR secs ,
it exits when a request falls due
.I secs
seconds or more after it started.
This limit is checked only when a request is due, so it no longer
applies once
.B \-c
requests have been sent.
.PP
In each of these cases,
.B ping
prints the statistics before it exits.
Other ways of ending it do not; see
.BR NOTES .
.SS Output
Output is on standard output.
The first line names the host, its address, and the size of the data
in each request:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
PING \fIhost\fR (\fIaddress\fR): \fIsize\fR data bytes
.EE
.RE
.PP
Each reply prints a line:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
\fIn\fR bytes from \fIaddress\fR: seq=\fIseq\fR ttl=\fIttl\fR time=\fIms\fR ms
.EE
.RE
.PP
.I n
is the size of the ICMP message, which is the data and an 8-byte
header.
.I seq
is the sequence number of the request, counting from 0.
.I ttl
is the time to live of the reply, or for IPv6, its hop limit.
.I ms
is the round-trip time in milliseconds, to the microsecond.
The time travels in the first 4 bytes of the data, so with fewer than 4
bytes of data the
.B time=
field is left out.
A reply to a request that has already been answered ends with
.B (DUP!)\&
and is counted as a duplicate.
.PP
The statistics follow a blank line:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
\-\-\- \fIhost\fR ping statistics \-\-\-
\fIsent\fR packets transmitted, \fIrecv\fR packets received, \fIloss\fR% packet loss
round\-trip min/avg/max = \fImin\fR/\fIavg\fR/\fImax\fR ms
.EE
.RE
.PP
When there were duplicates, the second line also gives their number,
as
.IR dup " duplicates,"
before the packet loss.
The loss is the percentage of requests with no reply, rounded down.
The round-trip line appears only when some reply had a time, and the
average includes the duplicates.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-qc4 ,
and an option argument may be attached or be the next argument.
Options may also follow
.IR host ,
unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
Exactly one
.I host
is required.
Numbers are decimal, unless stated otherwise.
.TP
.B \-4
Use only IPv4 addresses for
.IR host .
.TP
.B \-6
Use only IPv6 addresses for
.IR host .
It overrides
.BR \-4 .
.TP
.BI "\-c " count
Send
.I count
requests and stop, as described under
.BR Stopping .
Duplicate replies do not count towards
.IR count .
The default, 0, means no limit.
.TP
.BI "\-s " size
Send
.I size
bytes of data in each request, from 0 to 65535.
The default is 56.
A size too large for Windows to send ends
.B ping
with
.BR "ping: sendto: 10040: WSA error" .
.TP
.BI "\-i " secs
Send a request every
.I secs
seconds.
The default is 1.
.I secs
may have a fraction, as in
.BR 0.2 ,
and a suffix:
.B s
for seconds,
.B m
for minutes,
.B h
for hours, or
.B d
for days.
A value above 2147 seconds counts as 2147.
.TP
.B \-A
Send the next request as soon as a reply arrives, without waiting for
the interval.
When no reply arrives, the next request is still sent after the
interval.
.TP
.BI "\-t " ttl
Set the time to live of IPv4 requests, from 1 to 255.
It applies to requests sent to a multicast address too.
The default, 0, leaves the Windows default.
A value above 255 is ignored, and so is
.B \-t
for IPv6.
.TP
.BI "\-W " secs
After the last of the
.B \-c
requests, wait
.I secs
seconds for a reply when none has arrived yet.
The default is 10, and 0 does not wait.
Without
.BR \-c ,
it has no effect.
.TP
.BI "\-w " secs
Stop sending after
.I secs
seconds, as described under
.BR Stopping .
The default, 0, means no limit.
A value above 2147 counts as 2147.
This also changes the exit status; see
.BR "EXIT STATUS" .
.TP
.B \-q
Print only the first line and the statistics.
Incompatible with
.BR \-v .
.TP
.BI "\-p " hex
Fill the data with the byte
.IR hex ,
a hexadecimal number from 0 to ff.
The default is 0.
The first 4 bytes of the data hold the send time instead.
.TP
.B \-v
Accepted, and has no effect.
Incompatible with
.BR \-q .
.TP
.B \-n
Accepted, and has no effect.
Addresses are always printed as numbers.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
.PP
An unknown option, a missing or invalid option argument, both
.B \-q
and
.BR \-v ,
or the wrong number of operands prints the usage on standard error and
exits with status 1.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, the first operand ends the options, so options after
.I host
are an error.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
At least one reply arrived, and with
.B \-w
and
.BR \-c ,
at least
.I count
replies arrived.
Or the usage was requested with
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.B 1
No reply arrived, or with
.B \-w
and
.BR \-c ,
fewer than
.I count
replies arrived.
Or an error: an invalid command line, a host that could not be
resolved, a socket that could not be created, or a request that could
not be sent.
The message is on standard error, prefixed with
.BR ping: ,
as in
.BR "ping: bad address \(aq\%example.invalid\(aq" .
.SH EXAMPLES
Send two requests:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ping \-c 2 8.8.8.8
PING 8.8.8.8 (8.8.8.8): 56 data bytes
64 bytes from 8.8.8.8: seq=0 ttl=114 time=15.350 ms
64 bytes from 8.8.8.8: seq=1 ttl=114 time=11.364 ms
\&
\-\-\- 8.8.8.8 ping statistics \-\-\-
2 packets transmitted, 2 packets received, 0% packet loss
round\-trip min/avg/max = 11.364/13.357/15.350 ms
.EE
.RE
.PP
Test whether a host answers within 2 seconds:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ping \-q \-c 1 \-W 2 192.168.1.1 >/dev/null && echo up
.EE
.RE
.PP
Ping for 30 seconds, five times a second, and show only the statistics:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ping \-q \-w 30 \-i 0.2 example.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Ping the IPv6 loopback address:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ping6 ::1
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run the Windows program instead.
The kit's launcher comes first in
.BR PATH ,
so name the program by its path:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ c:/windows/system32/ping \-n 1 example.com
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
.B ping
sends and receives through a raw ICMP socket.
Recent versions of Windows, such as Windows 11, let a process that is
not elevated send echo requests this way, but some versions require an
elevated process,
such as one started by
.BR su (1).
Otherwise
.B ping
fails with a message such as
.BR "ping: can\(aqt create raw socket: 10013: WSA error" .
Socket errors are reported by their Winsock error number, not a
description.
.PP
Windows has its own
.B ping.exe
in
.IR System32 .
The kit's
.I bin
directory comes first in
.B PATH
and has launchers named
.B ping.exe
and
.BR ping6.exe ,
so a bare
.B ping
runs the applet in
.B cmd.exe
too.
In the shell, setting
.B BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS
to
.B ping
does not help, because the
.B PATH
search finds the launcher, which runs the applet anyway; see
.BR busybox (1).
Name the Windows program by its path instead, as in
.BR EXAMPLES .
.PP
Ctrl-C prints the statistics and exits with the status described under
.BR "EXIT STATUS" .
Ctrl-Break, and
.BR kill (1)
or
.BR timeout (1),
end
.B ping
at once without statistics.
To bound a run and still get statistics, use
.B \-c
or
.BR \-w .
.PP
Round-trip times come from the Windows performance counter, so they
are accurate to the microsecond.
.PP
Differences from other versions of
.BR ping :
.IP \(bu 2
There is no
.B \-I
option to choose a source interface or address.
It is recognized, and
.B ping
exits with
.BR "ping: \-I not supported" .
.IP \(bu 2
ICMP error messages, such as Time Exceeded or Destination Unreachable,
are not reported.
A request answered by one counts as lost.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-t
does not set the hop limit of IPv6 requests.
.IP \(bu 2
.B "\-W 0"
does not wait at all after the last request.
.IP \(bu 2
The reply line has
.BR seq= ,
not
.BR icmp_seq= .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR kill (1),
.BR nc (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR su (1),
.BR timeout (1),
.BR wget (1)
